1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,840
On the nineteenth of July, 2024,

2
00:00:02,070 --> 00:00:07,000
a security company called CrowdStrike
sent out a routine update.

3
00:00:07,733 --> 00:00:11,753
Not a new program.
Just a small configuration file.

4
00:00:12,460 --> 00:00:14,420
Within hours, eight and a half million

5
00:00:14,650 --> 00:00:17,440
Windows computers had stopped
on a blue screen.

6
00:00:18,060 --> 00:00:20,120
Airlines grounded flights.

7
00:00:20,220 --> 00:00:22,020
Hospitals cancelled operations.

8
00:00:22,820 --> 00:00:24,780
A news channel went off the air.

9
00:00:25,305 --> 00:00:28,005
And the mistake behind it
was an ordinary one.

10
00:00:28,605 --> 00:00:31,245
The program expected
twenty pieces of data.

11
00:00:32,005 --> 00:00:33,365
The update gave it twenty-one.

12
00:00:34,493 --> 00:00:37,793
Your games and apps make mistakes
like that all the time.

13
00:00:38,413 --> 00:00:41,533
When they do, the game closes,
and you start it again.

14
00:00:42,143 --> 00:00:45,603
Same kind of bug. One closes a window.

15
00:00:45,943 --> 00:00:48,423
The other stops
eight and a half million machines.

16
00:00:49,347 --> 00:00:51,327
The difference isn't the mistake.

17
00:00:51,727 --> 00:00:53,567
It's where the code was running.

18
00:00:54,224 --> 00:00:57,384
So why can't one program
take over your computer...

19
00:00:57,584 --> 00:00:58,964
and why could this one?

20
00:01:01,168 --> 00:01:03,288
It hasn't always been like this.

21
00:01:03,973 --> 00:01:09,393
On a PC running DOS in the 1980s, nothing
in the processor stopped a program.

22
00:01:10,233 --> 00:01:13,953
It could write into any part of memory,
and talk straight to the hardware.

23
00:01:14,717 --> 00:01:16,717
One program writing somewhere it shouldn't

24
00:01:16,947 --> 00:01:20,817
could overwrite the operating system,
and the whole machine froze.

25
00:01:21,597 --> 00:01:26,877
The fix was to stop trusting programs, and
to make the processor do the distrusting.

26
00:01:27,989 --> 00:01:31,829
In 1982, Intel's 80286 processor

27
00:01:32,059 --> 00:01:35,749
introduced protected mode,
with four levels of privilege.

28
00:01:36,618 --> 00:01:40,118
They're usually drawn
as four rings, one inside another.

29
00:01:40,703 --> 00:01:42,953
Ring zero is in the centre.

30
00:01:43,163 --> 00:01:44,783
Ring three is on the outside.

31
00:01:45,451 --> 00:01:47,371
And the numbering runs backwards.

32
00:01:48,031 --> 00:01:50,811
The lower the number,
the more the code is allowed to do.

33
00:01:51,615 --> 00:01:55,535
Ring zero belongs to the kernel,
the core of the operating system.

34
00:01:56,275 --> 00:02:01,115
It hands out memory, decides which program
runs next, and talks to the hardware.

35
00:02:01,889 --> 00:02:05,789
Ring three is where your browser,
your games and your text editor run.

36
00:02:06,329 --> 00:02:08,249
This is called user mode.

37
00:02:08,963 --> 00:02:12,583
Windows, macOS and Linux
all divide the work this way.

38
00:02:13,283 --> 00:02:16,363
The kernel in ring zero,
your apps in ring three.

39
00:02:16,976 --> 00:02:20,596
Phones and Apple's newer Macs
use ARM chips instead.

40
00:02:21,016 --> 00:02:23,636
There, the levels
are called exception levels.

41
00:02:24,296 --> 00:02:28,256
Different name, same split:
one for the kernel, one for your apps.

42
00:02:29,559 --> 00:02:32,739
The processor always knows
which ring it's running in.

43
00:02:32,879 --> 00:02:34,179
And it checks.

44
00:02:34,947 --> 00:02:37,667
Some instructions only work in ring zero.

45
00:02:38,407 --> 00:02:42,407
Switching off interrupts, for example,
or changing how memory is mapped.

46
00:02:42,788 --> 00:02:46,688
When a program in ring three tries one,
the processor refuses.

47
00:02:47,588 --> 00:02:50,588
It raises an exception
and hands control to the kernel,

48
00:02:50,668 --> 00:02:52,328
which usually ends the program.

49
00:02:53,062 --> 00:02:54,542
Memory gets the same treatment.

50
00:02:54,772 --> 00:02:59,322
Every page of memory is marked
as either user or supervisor.

51
00:03:00,264 --> 00:03:04,584
A program in ring three that touches
a supervisor page doesn't get the data.

52
00:03:04,824 --> 00:03:06,464
It gets stopped.

53
00:03:07,167 --> 00:03:10,167
Your browser and your game
both run in ring three.

54
00:03:10,787 --> 00:03:14,567
What keeps them apart is that each
one gets its own map of memory.

55
00:03:15,167 --> 00:03:18,227
The other program's memory
simply isn't on it.

56
00:03:18,566 --> 00:03:22,066
So when a game crashes,
the damage stays inside the game.

57
00:03:22,606 --> 00:03:24,106
The kernel cleans up.

58
00:03:24,506 --> 00:03:26,286
Everything else carries on.

59
00:03:28,426 --> 00:03:30,446
Now look at CrowdStrike again.

60
00:03:31,066 --> 00:03:34,146
Its driver ran in ring zero,
right next to the kernel.

61
00:03:34,817 --> 00:03:38,357
Nothing above ring zero
can catch a mistake like that.

62
00:03:38,724 --> 00:03:41,384
So Windows did the only safe thing left.

63
00:03:42,104 --> 00:03:44,464
It stopped the whole machine, on purpose,

64
00:03:44,544 --> 00:03:47,524
rather than carry on with memory
it could no longer trust.

65
00:03:48,273 --> 00:03:50,133
That's what the blue screen is.

66
00:03:50,643 --> 00:03:53,803
And because the driver started
every time Windows did,

67
00:03:53,883 --> 00:03:56,083
every restart brought the crash back.

68
00:03:58,783 --> 00:04:00,563
But this raises a question.

69
00:04:01,363 --> 00:04:03,363
If your browser can't touch the hardware,

70
00:04:03,443 --> 00:04:06,903
how does it ever save a file,
or send anything over the network?

71
00:04:07,913 --> 00:04:09,433
It asks.

72
00:04:09,971 --> 00:04:13,111
The program puts its request
into the processor's registers.

73
00:04:13,991 --> 00:04:16,751
Which file. Where the data should go.

74
00:04:17,091 --> 00:04:18,291
How much of it.

75
00:04:18,540 --> 00:04:20,840
Then it runs one special instruction.

76
00:04:21,240 --> 00:04:23,320
On a modern PC, it's called SYSCALL.

77
00:04:24,526 --> 00:04:28,735
In that instant, the processor switches
from ring three to ring zero...

78
00:04:28,815 --> 00:04:31,435
but it doesn't jump
wherever the program wants.

79
00:04:31,695 --> 00:04:35,395
It jumps to one entry point
the kernel chose in advance.

80
00:04:36,209 --> 00:04:40,289
The kernel checks the request, does
the work, and drops back to ring three.

81
00:04:40,749 --> 00:04:43,189
The program never touched the disk itself.

82
00:04:43,987 --> 00:04:47,047
This is a system call,
and it happens constantly.

83
00:04:47,859 --> 00:04:49,599
We counted them on a laptop.

84
00:04:50,299 --> 00:04:53,159
A program that does nothing at all
still makes thirty.

85
00:04:53,931 --> 00:04:57,011
Firefox, starting up
and loading one Wikipedia page,

86
00:04:57,241 --> 00:05:00,831
made about six hundred and seventy
thousand in twenty seconds...

87
00:05:00,911 --> 00:05:03,811
even with our counting slowing it down.

88
00:05:04,416 --> 00:05:07,216
Every one of them
is a trip through the wall and back.

89
00:05:09,776 --> 00:05:11,396
Which leaves something odd.

90
00:05:11,896 --> 00:05:14,156
Four rings, and we've only needed two.

91
00:05:14,802 --> 00:05:16,982
What happened to rings one and two?

92
00:05:17,629 --> 00:05:20,289
The idea of rings is older than the PC.

93
00:05:21,329 --> 00:05:26,129
Multics, an operating system
from the late 1960s, had eight of them.

94
00:05:26,644 --> 00:05:28,644
The idea was layers of trust.

95
00:05:29,524 --> 00:05:31,264
The core of the system in the middle,

96
00:05:31,344 --> 00:05:34,824
less trusted services around it,
and programs on the outside.

97
00:05:35,745 --> 00:05:37,865
Intel drew the same picture.

98
00:05:38,465 --> 00:05:41,045
Rings one and two were meant
for the layers in between,

99
00:05:41,125 --> 00:05:43,305
like drivers and system services.

100
00:05:43,945 --> 00:05:46,645
Trusted more than your apps,
less than the kernel.

101
00:05:47,322 --> 00:05:49,142
Two things got in the way.

102
00:05:50,050 --> 00:05:52,430
First, systems like Unix and Windows NT

103
00:05:52,660 --> 00:05:55,810
were designed to run
on many kinds of processor.

104
00:05:56,338 --> 00:05:59,578
And the one split
every processor offered was two levels.

105
00:06:00,338 --> 00:06:02,838
One for the kernel,
one for everything else.

106
00:06:03,476 --> 00:06:06,456
Second, the x86 processor itself.

107
00:06:07,116 --> 00:06:10,336
When it maps memory,
each page knows only two states.

108
00:06:11,096 --> 00:06:12,636
User, or supervisor.

109
00:06:13,560 --> 00:06:15,880
Rings one and two count as supervisor.

110
00:06:16,140 --> 00:06:20,100
So a driver in ring one
could still touch the kernel's memory.

111
00:06:20,840 --> 00:06:22,940
The extra ring wouldn't protect much.

112
00:06:23,665 --> 00:06:26,225
A few systems used them anyway.

113
00:06:26,765 --> 00:06:31,825
OS/2 let some programs step into ring two,
just to talk to the hardware directly.

114
00:06:32,317 --> 00:06:34,917
And before processors had help
for running virtual machines,

115
00:06:35,147 --> 00:06:39,177
Xen and VirtualBox moved
the guest's kernel into ring one,

116
00:06:39,257 --> 00:06:42,597
so the system underneath
could keep ring zero to itself.

117
00:06:43,335 --> 00:06:48,115
In 2023, Intel proposed
removing rings one and two altogether.

118
00:06:48,850 --> 00:06:51,150
In December 2024, it dropped the plan.

119
00:06:51,380 --> 00:06:53,990
They're still in your processor.

120
00:06:54,630 --> 00:06:56,390
Almost nothing uses them.

121
00:06:58,790 --> 00:07:00,870
So far, this sounds reassuring.

122
00:07:01,610 --> 00:07:04,950
The rules are in the hardware,
and the hardware checks every access.

123
00:07:06,272 --> 00:07:08,692
In 2017, three teams of researchers,

124
00:07:08,922 --> 00:07:12,032
working separately,
found a way through that wall.

125
00:07:12,661 --> 00:07:14,581
They called it Meltdown.

126
00:07:14,921 --> 00:07:18,461
And to follow it, you need one trick
that modern processors play.

127
00:07:19,138 --> 00:07:21,658
A processor doesn't wait
for one instruction to finish

128
00:07:21,888 --> 00:07:23,498
before starting the next.

129
00:07:24,318 --> 00:07:27,758
It runs ahead,
on instructions it will probably need.

130
00:07:28,152 --> 00:07:32,532
If it guessed wrong, it throws the results
away, as if nothing had happened.

131
00:07:33,555 --> 00:07:35,185
Here's the flaw.

132
00:07:35,355 --> 00:07:38,195
On affected Intel chips,
a program in ring three could ask

133
00:07:38,425 --> 00:07:39,915
for a byte of kernel memory...

134
00:07:40,171 --> 00:07:43,791
and the processor would fetch it
and pass it on to the next instructions

135
00:07:44,021 --> 00:07:46,991
before it had finished checking
whether that was allowed.

136
00:07:47,734 --> 00:07:51,094
The check did fail.
The result was thrown away.

137
00:07:51,843 --> 00:07:54,883
But those next instructions
had already used the secret byte

138
00:07:54,963 --> 00:07:57,683
to pull one piece
of the program's own memory

139
00:07:57,913 --> 00:07:59,663
into the processor's cache.

140
00:08:00,144 --> 00:08:03,904
And the cache doesn't forget,
just because the result was cancelled.

141
00:08:04,877 --> 00:08:08,657
So the program times
how fast each piece of its memory answers.

142
00:08:09,557 --> 00:08:12,277
The one that comes back fast
gives away the secret byte.

143
00:08:13,359 --> 00:08:16,039
One byte at a time,
an ordinary program could read

144
00:08:16,269 --> 00:08:19,739
the kernel's memory,
and whatever secrets were sitting in it.

145
00:08:20,359 --> 00:08:22,799
Passwords and encryption keys included.

146
00:08:25,199 --> 00:08:29,359
Meltdown affected Intel processors
going back to 1995.

147
00:08:30,415 --> 00:08:33,875
It was made public
on the third of January, 2018.

148
00:08:34,607 --> 00:08:36,747
The fix couldn't change the silicon.

149
00:08:37,247 --> 00:08:39,907
So operating systems changed the map.

150
00:08:40,293 --> 00:08:41,713
While your program runs,

151
00:08:41,793 --> 00:08:45,373
the kernel's memory is now taken off
its map almost entirely.

152
00:08:46,293 --> 00:08:47,913
There's nothing there to fetch.

153
00:08:48,647 --> 00:08:50,567
But remember those system calls.

154
00:08:51,347 --> 00:08:54,727
Every trip through the wall
now has to swap the map as well.

155
00:08:55,316 --> 00:08:59,296
Red Hat measured the patches for Meltdown
and a related flaw, Spectre.

156
00:08:59,386 --> 00:09:01,936
Under one percent on some work...

157
00:09:02,016 --> 00:09:04,416
up to nineteen on busy databases.

158
00:09:05,270 --> 00:09:07,110
The rule was right all along.

159
00:09:07,550 --> 00:09:09,770
The processor just checked it too late.

160
00:09:12,830 --> 00:09:16,270
Which brings us back to CrowdStrike,
and a harder question.

161
00:09:17,001 --> 00:09:20,661
Why was a security program
in ring zero in the first place?

162
00:09:21,121 --> 00:09:23,241
Because from there, it sees everything.

163
00:09:24,081 --> 00:09:26,361
It starts before other software does.

164
00:09:27,081 --> 00:09:30,721
And malware with administrator rights
has a harder time switching it off.

165
00:09:31,333 --> 00:09:34,073
Microsoft's own list ends with a warning.

166
00:09:34,673 --> 00:09:36,353
They come at the cost of resilience.

167
00:09:37,319 --> 00:09:40,299
After the outage,
Microsoft started building a way

168
00:09:40,529 --> 00:09:43,599
for antivirus software
to run outside the kernel.

169
00:09:44,220 --> 00:09:48,220
By autumn 2026,
it's still in test versions of Windows.

170
00:09:48,460 --> 00:09:51,880
And the kernel hasn't been closed
to security companies.

171
00:09:54,450 --> 00:09:56,490
Your games face the same dilemma.

172
00:09:57,405 --> 00:10:00,265
Cheats that run in the kernel
are invisible to an anti-cheat

173
00:10:00,345 --> 00:10:02,425
that runs in ring three.

174
00:10:02,783 --> 00:10:04,943
So the anti-cheat moved in too.

175
00:10:05,643 --> 00:10:09,543
Riot's Vanguard, used by Valorant,
puts a driver into ring zero.

176
00:10:10,283 --> 00:10:13,123
On most PCs, it loads as Windows starts...

177
00:10:13,423 --> 00:10:15,283
even if you never open the game.

178
00:10:15,918 --> 00:10:17,478
That doesn't make it faulty.

179
00:10:17,658 --> 00:10:21,478
But it sits in the same place
the CrowdStrike driver did,

180
00:10:21,558 --> 00:10:23,998
where one mistake stops the whole machine.

181
00:10:26,748 --> 00:10:29,688
So why can't one program
take over your computer?

182
00:10:30,313 --> 00:10:33,553
Because the processor keeps
almost every program out of the centre,

183
00:10:33,633 --> 00:10:36,333
and checks them every time they knock.

184
00:10:36,980 --> 00:10:39,960
But the processor can't decide
who gets let in.

185
00:10:40,240 --> 00:10:43,940
People decide that...
every time they install a driver.

186
00:10:45,251 --> 00:10:47,291
So here's a question for the comments.

187
00:10:48,071 --> 00:10:50,971
Who should decide which programs
get into ring zero:

188
00:10:51,051 --> 00:10:53,661
you, Microsoft,
or the companies that make them?
